# LF line endings

Repository policy: [`.gitattributes`](.gitattributes) sets `* text eol=lf`. Every file you create or rewrite must use **LF** (`\n`), never **CRLF** (`\r\n`).

## Hard rules

1. Write tools and editors must emit LF. Do not rely on Windows defaults.
2. After creating or bulk-editing text files, verify no `\r\n` was introduced.
3. Do not change an existing file's line-ending style unless the task requires it.
4. Binary files are exempt; everything else is text unless proven otherwise.

## Windows pitfalls

PowerShell defaults to CRLF. Avoid:

```powershell
# BAD — often writes CRLF
Set-Content -Path file.txt -Value $text
Out-File -FilePath file.txt -InputObject $text
```

Prefer the Write/StrReplace editor tools (they preserve LF when the file uses LF). When shell output must land in a file:

```powershell
# GOOD — explicit LF
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText("file.txt", $text.Replace("`r`n", "`n"))
```

Or normalize after write:

```powershell
$p = "file.txt"
$c = (Get-Content -Raw $p) -replace "`r`n", "`n"
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($p, $c)
```

## Quick check

```powershell
git diff --check
rg -l "`r" --glob '!*.png' --glob '!*.jpg' .
```

Fix any CRLF before commit.
